# G043 — Zip / Postal Code Lookup

*v2. Closes Networking. See `zip-lookup.md` for prose: third identity-
to-context layer (after city→coords, IP→country), postal codes as
hierarchical identifiers, radius search as proximity discovery,
multiple indexes serve different access patterns.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Location-Aware Delivery

[shipping-estimate @origin-code @destination-code
    IN ORDER TO
        resolve both postal codes, compute great-circle distance
        and find nearby warehouses within a delivery radius
        only when both endpoints and at least one warehouse resolve

    @origin      <- [postal[lookup {code: @origin-code}]]
    @destination <- [postal[lookup {code: @destination-code}]]

    @origin and @destination
        where both are not none
            @distance <- [geo[haversine
                                {from: @origin:coords,
                                 to: @destination:coords}]]
            @warehouses <- [postal[radius
                                     {center: @destination-code,
                                      km: 200}]]
            @warehouses
                where count is at least 1
                    -> [compose
                         {distance: @distance,
                          warehouses: @warehouses}]
                otherwise
                    -> #no-warehouse-coverage
        otherwise
            -> #unresolved-postal-code
                {missing: [@origin-code, @destination-code]
                          where resolution is none}
]

---

## Verb Surface

[postal
    lookup              {code}                    ;; -> Record | none
    by-city             {city, state}              ;; -> [Record]
    by-state            {state}                     ;; -> [Record]
    radius              {center, km}                ;; -> [Record]
    hierarchy           {code}                      ;; -> {region, sectional, area}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: hierarchy from prefix `where` digits encode scope

[postal[hierarchy {code: @c}]
    IN ORDER TO
        decompose a US zip into its layered geographic scopes
        so region / sectional / area all become first-class fields

    @c
        where length is 5 and digits only
            -> {region:    first digit,
                sectional: first three digits,
                area:      all five digits}
        otherwise
            -> #invalid-zip-format
]

Same shape InnateScript's namespace uses: `@forge.engineering.testing`
decomposes into forge / engineering / testing. The resolver could
route by prefix the way postal routing does — region first, area last.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: multiple indexes `where` access pattern varies

[postal[indexes]
    by-code:   @code → Record          (exact lookup, O(1))
    by-city:   (city, state) → [Record] (reverse lookup, O(1))
    by-state:  state → [Record]         (regional aggregation)
    by-coord:  geo-grid → [Record]      (radius queries)

    each index serves a different query pattern
    same data, different entry points — the resolver viewed from
    multiple angles is G076 Bookmarks' multi-axis organisation
    applied to postal records
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [postal[lookup {code: "32202"}]]
    ;; -> {code: "32202", city: "Jacksonville", state: "FL",
    ;;     coords: (30.33, -81.66)}

    -> [postal[hierarchy {code: "32202"}]]
    ;; -> {region: 3, sectional: 322, area: 32202}

    -> [postal[by-city {city: "Jacksonville", state: "FL"}]]
    ;; -> [multiple zips serving Jacksonville]

    -> [postal[radius {center: "32202", km: 50}]]
    ;; -> [nearby zips within 50km]
]
